## Framing: claude

### Framing
The z-harness is a collection of commands that each re-implement the same three-phase lifecycle — *fan out* (spawn N parallel subagents along some axis), *gate* (check outputs against thresholds and escalate), and *reconcile* (merge heterogeneous artifacts). Proposal: extract a `z-fanout` FGR (Fan-out / Gate / Reconcile) primitive that makes this lifecycle a first-class, parameterizable abstraction.

### Core hypothesis
A single `fan-out-gate-reconcile` script library with three parameters: (a) strategy axis (dimensions/vendors/tasks/clusters), (b) gate spec (threshold table → escalation targets), (c) reconciliation contract — typed merge-strategy names: `dedup-score`, `anti-bias-merge`, `task-append`, `delta-patch`. Every command becomes a thin wrapper that defines its axis + gate + contract.

### Risks
- Over-abstraction obscures domain semantics.
- Reconciliation heterogeneity may require a plugin mechanism as complex as the current per-command logic.
- Escalation targets differ per command — risk of circular escalation chains.
- Testing surface explodes (cartesian product of axes × gates × contracts).
- Strategy axes are structurally different (vendor = symmetric / no deps; task = dependency-constrained DAG).

### Plan implications
- Bash script library `scripts/fanout-lib.sh`.
- Per-command `ESCALATION_SPEC` env blocks.
- Typed merge-strategy dispatch table.
- Migration order: `/z-audit` and `/z-brainstorm` first (cleanest shapes); `/z-implement-all` last (most specialized).
- Gate-spec format is the load-bearing decision.

### What would change my mind
- <30% shared code surface across commands.
- Branch outputs differ so much that typed merge-strategy can't be normalized without quality loss.
- `/z-implement-all` needs >2 special-case hooks — scope it out of the primitive entirely.

